1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
Let’s walk through Company Memory by AI 4 SALE.

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:07,225
The problem is familiar: you start a new chat, and suddenly you’re

3
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:09,540
explaining the project all over again.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,325
Here are our eight stops: connect the memory, understand the project, do

5
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:19,165
real work, bring in a teammate, update a decision, review and share

6
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:23,080
information, remove an old note, and control access.

7
00:00:23,667 --> 00:00:27,287
We’ll follow Aurora, our demo website, between Codex and ChatGPT.

8
00:00:27,927 --> 00:00:31,312
This is the account portal, where I choose which shared memory this

9
00:00:31,327 --> 00:00:32,267
connection can use.

10
00:00:33,267 --> 00:00:35,807
A zone separates one area of knowledge from another.

11
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:37,987
General holds our project information;

12
00:00:38,527 --> 00:00:40,947
the restricted Aurora General zone stays separate.

13
00:00:41,547 --> 00:00:44,627
Sharing project context doesn’t mean opening every part of the business.

14
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:48,687
For this connection, I allow reading and recording useful results.

15
00:00:49,227 --> 00:00:51,747
The owner also has the controls we’ll use later.

16
00:00:52,287 --> 00:00:56,087
A teammate can have fewer permissions, even when using the same memory.

17
00:00:56,727 --> 00:00:58,767
A writing agent can record results;

18
00:00:59,047 --> 00:01:01,687
a reporting assistant may only need to read them.

19
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:03,647
Now we’re connected.

20
00:01:04,287 --> 00:01:07,687
Another app can use the same memory without creating a separate copy.

21
00:01:08,127 --> 00:01:10,367
From here, I can ask ordinary work questions.

22
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:13,760
First, I ask what’s happening with Aurora.

23
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:18,280
Codex looks up the project context and gives me a source-backed answer.

24
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:22,280
I can inspect the source instead of just trusting a confident summary.

25
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:26,305
That matters: it tells us what was agreed, not just what sounds

26
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:26,920
plausible.

27
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,220
The scope is a one-page website with a contact form.

28
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:35,125
We agree on the button, the follow-up owner and why online payments

29
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:37,960
are out. I ask for a developer brief;

30
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:40,580
the useful outcome is recorded automatically.

31
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:45,818
Notice the difference between a request being processed and a result
being

32
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:50,078
available. Once the outcome is accepted, another permitted client can
read

33
00:01:50,093 --> 00:01:52,933
it. The next scene checks exactly that.

34
00:01:54,533 --> 00:01:56,633
Here’s a fresh Codex CLI session.

35
00:01:57,173 --> 00:02:00,158
I ask it to apply the agreed change, but I don’t paste

36
00:02:00,173 --> 00:02:02,973
the earlier conversation or repeat the button label.

37
00:02:03,513 --> 00:02:05,513
It needs the shared project knowledge.

38
00:02:06,067 --> 00:02:07,287
The change is small on purpose.

39
00:02:07,367 --> 00:02:11,172
We can see the actual edit, rather than just a plan saying

40
00:02:11,187 --> 00:02:12,147
what should happen.

41
00:02:13,147 --> 00:02:14,527
Codex is doing the development;

42
00:02:14,807 --> 00:02:17,147
Company Memory supplies the project context.

43
00:02:17,487 --> 00:02:22,112
The before-and-after view lets us check the change, rather than rely on

44
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:23,707
the assistant saying it has finished.

45
00:02:24,633 --> 00:02:25,733
Now let’s look at the page.

46
00:02:25,873 --> 00:02:27,993
The button says ‘Book a walkthrough’.

47
00:02:28,013 --> 00:02:30,433
I click it, and the contact form appears.

48
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:32,533
That’s the behavior we asked for.

49
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:38,805
The useful result is recorded automatically, including what was checked

50
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:39,980
and what wasn’t done.

51
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,240
This is a local change, not a public deployment.

52
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:45,520
There’s no second ‘save this’ message.

53
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:49,233
Now someone else needs to report on the work.

54
00:02:49,713 --> 00:02:53,113
Normally, that means finding the right chat and writing another recap.

55
00:02:53,713 --> 00:02:56,453
Instead, I invite a teammate to the same project memory.

56
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:00,060
They join with their own account.

57
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,305
This ChatGPT connection can read General, but it can’t change the shared

58
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,265
records. Each person has their own account, and each connection has its

59
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:12,185
own permissions. We’re sharing project knowledge, not the owner’s login

60
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:13,560
or original conversation.

61
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:17,700
In ChatGPT, I ask for a progress update.

62
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:21,040
The answer combines the agreed decision with the work just completed.

63
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:25,145
It separates what was planned, tested locally and not deployed without

64
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:26,300
inventing a launch date.

65
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:32,700
Here’s the problem with remembering only the original decision: plans
change.

66
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,180
We now want ‘Request pilot access’, and Maya will handle enquiries.

67
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:39,220
The rest of the agreement stays the same.

68
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:40,740
Maya handles enquiries;

69
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:42,280
Nia remains the project owner.

70
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:43,860
Those are different responsibilities.

71
00:03:45,033 --> 00:03:47,273
The existing decision is being updated.

72
00:03:47,853 --> 00:03:51,078
While the check is still running, it isn’t ready to use as

73
00:03:51,093 --> 00:03:52,173
the accepted version.

74
00:03:52,653 --> 00:03:55,733
We confirm availability before moving to the other client.

75
00:03:57,033 --> 00:03:58,913
Now a fresh ChatGPT conversation.

76
00:03:59,493 --> 00:04:03,038
I ask what the button should say and who handles enquiries, without

77
00:04:03,053 --> 00:04:04,593
supplying either new value.

78
00:04:05,013 --> 00:04:07,833
The answer brings back ‘Request pilot access’ and Maya.

79
00:04:08,633 --> 00:04:11,333
That updates the shared plan, not the website itself.

80
00:04:11,813 --> 00:04:14,133
The earlier implementation remains earlier work.

81
00:04:14,473 --> 00:04:17,713
Remembering a new requirement is not the same as implementing it.

82
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,080
Sometimes you need to know why today’s answer differs from yesterday’s.

83
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:24,900
Here I ask for the plan’s history.

84
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,840
We can follow the recorded changes instead of relying on somebody’s
recollection.

85
00:04:31,033 --> 00:04:35,278
Next, the designer needs the brief and latest decision not access to

86
00:04:35,293 --> 00:04:36,353
every project record.

87
00:04:36,853 --> 00:04:38,653
I ask for both in one file.

88
00:04:39,153 --> 00:04:42,513
The assistant retrieves those selected records and prepares the handoff.

89
00:04:42,993 --> 00:04:46,618
This is a snapshot for the designer, not ongoing permission to browse

90
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:47,593
the team’s memory.

91
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:52,340
I open the file and check the current button text and owner.

92
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:56,965
The designer gets the selected information in one place, without needing

93
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:00,000
to navigate our workspace or read through the original conversation.

94
00:05:01,967 --> 00:05:04,287
Not everything needs to stay in memory forever.

95
00:05:05,507 --> 00:05:10,452
This temporary recording note has served its purpose, so I ask to

96
00:05:10,467 --> 00:05:14,527
remove it. We’re deleting one specific note, not the project.

97
00:05:15,433 --> 00:05:18,153
The response confirms the note is no longer available through memory.

98
00:05:18,793 --> 00:05:22,453
I then open Aurora’s current decision: it’s still there.

99
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:26,293
Removing old clutter doesn’t mean losing the knowledge we still need.

100
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:30,020
Finally, let’s check the limits.

101
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:32,940
Seventeen times twenty-four is just a calculation.

102
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:36,245
It needs no project context, so Company Memory doesn’t need to be

103
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:36,680
involved.

104
00:05:38,067 --> 00:05:41,832
Back in the read-only connection, I try to add online checkout to

105
00:05:41,847 --> 00:05:46,272
the plan. The request does not override the permission: this teammate
can

106
00:05:46,287 --> 00:05:48,647
read the project, but cannot change it.

107
00:05:49,367 --> 00:05:52,852
The supplier quote belongs to the restricted Aurora General zone,
outside

108
00:05:52,867 --> 00:05:53,887
this connection’s access.

109
00:05:54,147 --> 00:05:56,767
The assistant doesn’t reveal it or guess the price.

110
00:05:57,407 --> 00:06:00,087
Shared memory still means selectively shared knowledge.

111
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:03,800
I can also revoke just this ChatGPT connection.

112
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,965
That’s useful when I stop using an app or want to remove

113
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:09,440
its access without disrupting everyone else.

114
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:12,600
On its next request, it can no longer read the project.

115
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:15,720
Another authorized connection still retrieves the decision;

116
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:17,900
the knowledge hasn’t been deleted.

117
00:06:19,933 --> 00:06:23,598
So we’ve carried one project through a real change, a handoff and

118
00:06:23,613 --> 00:06:27,678
a revised decision, without passing the original chat around or asking

119
00:06:27,693 --> 00:06:28,873
for a separate save.

120
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:32,765
For the exact steps, open the user guide at

121
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:35,120
memory dot A I four dot sale.

122
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:39,260
It covers setup, permissions and the workflows we’ve just followed.
